# Suppressions for the release vulnerability gate (LOCI-044, §9.2).
#
# THE GATE IS ON FIXABLE CRITICAL AND HIGH, ANYWHERE IN THE IMAGE (🧭 3). It
# does not exempt the upstream PHP base: the largest CVE surface here is
# ImageMagick and its delegate stack, which LaraOCI installs itself (D13), and
# --ignore-unfixed already handles the "upstream has no fix yet" case. What
# remains is a fixable base CVE, and the control for that is §9.3's weekly
# rebuild against a current base - not an exemption.
#
# So an entry here is an admission that a release is shipping with a known,
# fixable CRITICAL or HIGH. That is sometimes the right call. It is never the
# right call indefinitely, which is why:
#
#   EVERY ENTRY CARRIES AN EXPIRY DATE, and the release notes list the ones
#   that were active for that release.
#
# Format (Trivy honours the `exp:` field):
#
#   CVE-2026-12345 exp:2026-09-01   # why, and what unblocks it
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Active entries. Each one is listed in the release notes of every release it
# is active for (LOCI-047), so a suppression cannot quietly outlive its reason.

# REMOVED 2026-08-12: CVE-2026-64561 and CVE-2026-64564 (linux-libc-dev).
#
# The gate's first real block, on the 🚦 Gate 2 staging run of 2026-08-11: two
# Linux KERNEL flaws (KVM, SCTP) reported against the kernel HEADERS package.
# Suppressed at the time because the fix looked unobtainable - first blamed on
# Debian not having published 6.12.101-1, then on a GHA cache hit keeping the
# apt layer from re-running.
#
# Both diagnoses were wrong, and the second was disproved by deleting every
# cache entry in the repository: the cold rebuild produced 6.12.100-1 again.
# linux-libc-dev ships INSTALLED in the upstream base (6.12.100-1 on php:8.3,
# 8.4 and 8.5-fpm-trixie alike) and nothing in the graph upgraded an
# already-installed package, so the version was whatever upstream last shipped,
# cache or no cache, forever.
#
# runtime's RUN 1 now runs `apt-get -y upgrade` (D43), measured at 1 package and
# +14.3 kB, which takes it to 6.12.101-1 and fixes both. So there is nothing
# left to suppress. See D40, D41 and D43 for the full record - including that
# D41's cache hazard is real, still applies to §9.3's weekly rebuild, and was
# never what held these two.

# builder, 2026-08-11. npm's BUNDLED dependency tree, under
# /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules - not node itself, and not
# anything LaraOCI chose.
#
# These three are what survives after doing the fixable part. `builder` now
# upgrades npm to 12.0.2 (D42), which took the set from seven findings to these
# three and cleared the CRITICAL node-tar gzip bomb - the one that mattered
# most, since `npm ci` is what extracts untrusted tarballs (D26). Bumping the
# node digest was measured first and fixes nothing: the newest
# node:lts-trixie-slim carries the identical seven.
#
# What remains is fixable in the npm REGISTRY and not obtainable in any release:
# no published npm bundles brace-expansion 5.0.8/5.0.9 or ip-address 10.3.1 yet,
# so --ignore-unfixed does not exclude them and no version of node or npm clears
# them. All three are denial-of-service in a BUILD image, not the runtime ones.
#
# What unblocks them: an npm release carrying the newer bundles. Re-check by
# scanning node:lts-trixie-slim directly - if they are gone there, drop these
# and raise the pin above.
CVE-2026-14257 exp:2026-09-15   # brace-expansion 5.0.7 (npm 12.0.2), needs 5.0.8
CVE-2026-69152 exp:2026-09-15   # brace-expansion 5.0.7 (npm 12.0.2), needs 5.0.9
CVE-2026-69192 exp:2026-09-15   # ip-address 10.2.0, needs 10.3.1
